# Build reasonable things, not spaceships

<!-- core -->
**Build the simplest reasonable thing.** Cheap, well-known good practice is part of the requirement
and is done without being asked: an index behind a query, a bounded read, paging in the database, no
N+1, batched writes, a timeout on an outbound call, a reusable object built once. Elaborate machinery
is not: speculative abstraction, configurability or cases nobody asked for wait until the user asks.
Always on, every project and every subagent.
So is whether it still works with two app instances, a Redis Cluster or ten times the data.

**Correctness, security and stated requirements always win** — when the simple version genuinely
won't do, say why in one sentence and build the one that works. If what you are building outgrows the
requirement mid-task, cut it back then rather than refactoring later.

<!-- /core -->

The test for machinery is the requirement, not a judgment about what might be needed later. A design
handling cases nobody asked for isn't more robust — it's more code to read, more state to keep
consistent, and more surface for a defect to hide in. A comment naming a ceiling does not excuse
skipping the cheap practice that removes it.

## The specific habits

- **Derive state; don't store it.** A value computable from what you already have needs no field, no
  `remember`, no state holder, no synchronisation rule. Storing it creates a second source of truth
  and the obligation to keep both agreeing.
- **No abstraction until there is a second caller.** One implementation needs no interface, strategy,
  registry or factory. Add the seam when the second case arrives and shows you where it belongs.
- **No configurability nobody asked for.** A hard-coded value that is right beats an option never set
  to anything else.
- **No speculative cases.** Don't handle inputs, states or failure modes the requirement doesn't
  mention and the code can't produce.
- **Fewer moving parts beats fewer lines.** Simple means less to reason about, not tersest syntax. A
  clear loop beats a clever one-liner.

## The limits

This rule governs the space where several correct designs exist and one is smaller — it is never licence
to under-build or ignore a real constraint.
